{-
   What `@inline` and `@capacity` refuse - Implementation-Containers.md §7.

   Three of §7.1's four rows are not built, and the refusal is a diagnostic rather than a silent
   fallback to the plain array. That is the point of the annotations being ABI: a field whose layout
   quietly stopped being what was written is the one failure a Repr refinement must not have.

   # resolver: ownership
-}

import Native

-- `@inline(i)` alone spills, and spilling needs the discriminant that says whether the inline bytes
-- hold elements or a pointer to them.
data Spilling {xs: @inline(4) [Int]}

-- `@capacity(c)` alone is always external, which needs the same field to be a narrowed capacity
-- rather than absent.
data Bounded {xs: @capacity(15) [Int]}

-- And the two together at different numbers, which is the row that has both a spill and a bound.
data Small {xs: @inline(4) @capacity(15) [Int]}

-- A refinement of something that is not a container. `@inline` is a statement about where a
-- container's elements live, so a type with none has nothing for it to say.
fn notAContainer(x: @inline(4) @capacity(4) Int) -> Int = x

-- An array that can hold nothing is not a smaller array, it is a different mistake: `[T]` is what an
-- array that allocates is spelled.
data Empty {xs: @inline(0) @capacity(0) [Int]}

-- A literal longer than `@capacity(n)` moved to `Reject.InlineCapacity.Legacy.yana`; that
-- file says what the new stage does instead.

fn main() -> Int = 0
